und the place and  particularly in Germany where people are  correctly concerned with their security  and things and I think that's a very  interesting development now another key  technology that we have been talking  about recently a lot is Wolfram language  we launched this as a separate sort of  items so to speak our last year really  Wolfram language has been brewing for  much longer than that because it's been  inside Mathematica but what we've done  is turn this into its own standalone  type of language and it's worth  remembering just you know what it what  it offers you know it's literate so you  can immediately see what you've written  much more than most languages it's sort  of multifaceted but unified and it's  also you know it was in an era already  when it when it first started emerging  we're kind of like the human trumps the  computer it's like very early languages  you're so worried about the power of the  computer that you made the language very  close to what the computer understood  and the problem with that is it makes it  hard for the human but we're in a modern  world where frankly  for the most part computer power is kind  of more than we need in many cases and  so not always but in many cases and so  it's more important designer language  that works well for humans even if it's  a little bit more effort for the  computer and of course then you want to  optimize it anyway so that in fact the  computer runs fast and often after a  time you can optimize it sufficiently  that you can actually do really really  well in making it just as fast as it  would have been if it was sort of very  close to the computer so the principal  who has been that and of course the key  thing is that the language is very much  attached to computation and so the  computations kind of ready to use that  and that seems a crucial thing and I  know over the last year in particular  people have really been latching on to  the idea of the Wolfram language and  thinking about Wolfram language in  comparison to other programming  languages not just to other computation  software and so we've had a lot of  people talking about how to use it  instead of Python and instead of Java  and instead of other things of that sort  where they hadn't really thought of it  as a language before and I think the  power of that will grow more particular  as we have the cloud applications  growing more one of things you may have  seen in the last year is tweeter program  and this was a program to be able to  tweet and maybe use some of you have  done this um the idea is that we have  such a powerful language that in 140  characters you can actually tweet a  meaningful program and so as you see  there have been 12 and a half thousand  nearly tweets and these are all the  programs people have come up with that  work in 140 characters so I think that's  a kind of a nice demonstration that we  have a highly compact language from  which you can do a huge amount in a very  sort of short short run now on the  challenges we've had is that we have an  awful lot of emerging technologies many  of you will be familiar in particular  being keen users for a long time of  Mathematica and the core technology  there underlies all of these other  things but actually we have many of  these different pieces emerging for  different uses for different sets of  people with different functionality and  so we have all these products emerging  up  and the project we've now embarking on  is how do we piece these together into  an ultimate computation system so we've  got the ultimate underlying piece we've  got the core unification of the language  and we've got many other pieces that are  very unified but we also want product  ization that is useful for many  different groups of people and so the  key sort of question is how do you piece  this together to get in a sense both  ubiquity and unification and one of the  things we've been thinking about for a  long long time I've shown this diagram  before is when you product ties things  do you make them discrete products or do  you make them sort of a continuous wave  of products across everything and we've  always believed that you should have  this sort of unification so you know you  can look at these different things there  were graph and network systems there are  high speed numeric system these are  peaks that in a sense we're above our  technological level but over time we try  and submerge these peaks and you know  the idea is that over time our overall  system actually can do better even at  the specialized ends of the spectrum but  even if it doesn't quite hit on some  polly particular area it's very you  don't have to build too much on top of  our system in order to be able to  supplant the the very specialized areas  and one of the things to notice if you  are in a specialized system kind of you  know on the top of a peak and you want  to do something just a little bit  different than what's on the peak it's a  long way to fall if you're in  traditional system as in you've got a  long way and it's also rather  uncomfortable sitting on a spike um so  it's very important I think to have this  sort of continuous functionality across  all of these areas of computation now  one of the problems with piecing these  things together is that it's harder for  us than anyone else why do I say that  because on the other hand the power we  can get out of that is also dramatically  more and it's a unique opportunity well  the reason I say that is because of this  what I call jus allottees and  hybridizations so one of our problems is  which is great problem to have but it  also makes my life harder  is that we don't have discrete sort of  differences between let's say  development and deployment if you look  out there most systems there are either  development systems or their deployment  systems they're not kind of halfway in  between they don't do both all in one  because for us deployment and  developments are kind of mixed you can  deploy an application to somebody and  they can change the code and they can  reuse it or they can adjust some facet  of it in a way that would never be  imaginable in other systems you know you  deploy a document to somebody and that's  the document there are people who have  the document there are people who have  this system that's there and you are the  developer and they are the deploy the  person to whom it's deployed so we have  these sort of what I call it's a bit  like wave particle duality right it's  like it's neither a wave you know nor is  it a particle or you can say that  there's a mathematical description that  represents both and there's just some  way you have to overlay on top of this  to think about what it is is a human  it's a bit like that with all of these  and you know symbolics numerics they're  not distinct for us there's kind of mesh  together we have symbolic representation  of numerics we have arbitrary precision  numerics you know these things are not  distinct they're not discrete  computation and coding traditionally you  have computation systems and you have  languages and they're kind of separate  they're not for us they're all together  the cloud and local I just talked about  how that's hybridized in our model of it  and data and expressions so the way you  have to think about we have is we have  this huge kind of space this continuous  space in all of these dimensions and the  question is how to piece that together  optimally with the core basis that we  have so let's also talk about sort of  facets of ubiquity how do we dry  computation to every where it can go  well we need to automate the insides and  we've done that rather effectively in  many cases although not all you need to  get it running everywhere so everyone  can use it on any device I think the  cloud helps a great deal with that and  you need to have coverage across all the  areas that you want to cover you know do  we cover what we need in machine  learning do  cover what we need in statistics do we  cover what we need in interface building  all of these sorts of areas are  important in terms of coverage in order  to achieve that and also how is it  productized do people understand what  they can get when they do stuff with us  so the big question I've been working on  is how do we marry ubiquity and  unification that's a big ask and I think  I won't go into too much detail here but  one of the things you'll be seeing  emerging is we're trying to build a  superstructure of what I call workflows  workspaces platforms and components so  the idea is that you either as a sort of  end user of something we've built or  indeed for making something for your  yourself or your clients or your  organization will be able to construct  any sort of any take on any of these  elements any of these dualities in the  easiest possible way I think the way to  think about that is to think first about  what workflow you really want to achieve  what do you want to start with what do  you want to end up with and how do you  get between those two using the whole  some of the technology now one elements  of that in a sense is how you present it  and what the workspace is and so in a  sense being able to construct  nice-looking workspaces is an important  element of that we've already started  releasing some platforms and starting on  like finance platform for example and  data science platform is is coming and  the point of those platforms in a sense  is to collect together work flows into  manageable chunks that people can  understand if that's all they want at  that at that stage in a sense and then  the components are in a sense of the  underlying pieces of chunks of  technology that really service that so  in a sense those are the elements that  we are piecing together into a sort of  superstructure and I think you'll be  very exciting us as this emerges so one  of the things we're making sure as a  company and we haven't always done these  things particularly well there's this  expression in English eat your own dog  food it's not as bad as it sounds it's a  software Express  which means if you build stuff you  should use it yourself and we've done  this very successfully for our own  computation one thing we're driving out  internally to do much harder to use all  of our development technology to develop  everything we need to develop as much as  possible whether it's our own ERP system  or whether it's are you know searches in  email systems or whatever we use our own  technology as much as possible because  most of these rely on computation and as  much as possible we think we should use  our own technology because then we iron  out all that work flow issues that we  are talking about so we are getting  better its so called eating our own dog  food product izing our ecosystem is as I  talked about the other thing is we want  to demonstrate the unexpected one of the  problems with our technology that these  dualities I talked about is it's hard  for people who are not the sorts of  people you are many times coming and  visiting Ursa so it's hard to explain to  them what is it that we can so cleanly  do for you and so one of the things and  I'd love to have examples from you all  as you as you think of them is what I  call sort of pivotal examples unexpected  examples where people think my goodness  I didn't know that computation could do  that or I didn't know the wolf from  ecosystem could produce that it just  seems like things that are wired  together that you wouldn't expect that  seems surprising and something I haven't  talked about much here but is absolutely  central is being the ultimate  development platform you know many of  you develop things for other people or  for your own organizations or for  yourself and it's crucial that those  things can be fitted together well and  that you can produce any of those things  that you want as quickly as possible 